{title: O Sacred Head, Now Wounded}
{artist: Paul Gerhardt · tr. James W. Alexander}
{key: C}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 66}

Verse 1
O [C]sacred head, now [G]wounded, with [Am]grief and shame weighed [G]down,
now [C]scornfully sur[F]rounded with [G]thorns, thine only [C]crown:
o sacred head, what [G]glory, what [Am]bliss till now was [G]thine!
Yet, [C]though despised and [F]gory, I [G]joy to call thee [C]mine.

Verse 2
What [C]Thou, my Lord, [G]hast suffered, [Am]was all for sinners' [G]gain;
Mine, [C]mine was the [G]transgression, but Thine [C]the
deadly pain. Lo, here [G]I fall, my [Am]Savior! 'Tis I deserve [G]Thy
place; [C]Look on me [F]with Thy favor, [G]vouchsafe to me Thy [C]grace.

Verse 3
Men [C]mock and taunt [G]and jeer [Am]Thee, Thou noble countenance, [G]Though
mighty [C]worlds shall fear [G]Thee and flee [C]before
Thy glance. How art [G]thou pale with [Am]anguish, with sore abuse [G]and
scorn! [C]How doth Thy [F]visage languish that [G]once was bright as [C]morn!

Verse 4
Now [C]from Thy cheeks [G]has vanished their [Am]color once so fair; [G]From
Thy [C]red lips is [G]banished the splendor that [C]was
there. Grim death, with [G]cruel rigor, hath [Am]robbed Thee of Thy [G]life;
Thus [C]Thou hast lost [F]Thy vigor, Thy [G]strength in this sad [C]strife.

Verse 5
My [C]burden in Thy [G]Passion, Lord, Thou [Am]hast borne for me, [G]For
it [C]was my transgression [G]which brought this [C]woe
on Thee. I cast [G]me down [Am]before Thee, wrath were [G]my
rightful [C]lot; Have mercy, [F]I implore [G]Thee; Redeemer, spurn me [C]not!
